[{:db/id 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b83d51a4-64e8-5d15-b4eb-1a14ddbe497b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b83d51a4-64e8-5d15-b4eb-1a14ddbe497b" :question/id #uuid "b83d51a4-64e8-5d15-b4eb-1a14ddbe497b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81851" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}a & 3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & b \\\\ 7 & c & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9be8ac92-24b2-5071-9ee2-8d0a8572a03d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9be8ac92-24b2-5071-9ee2-8d0a8572a03d" :answer-field/id #uuid "9be8ac92-24b2-5071-9ee2-8d0a8572a03d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-38d68fa8-7de7-5c1e-a154-a690cae0ed7e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-90c0ac77-fbcb-52ce-b243-0e75ec356a91" "answer-38d68fa8-7de7-5c1e-a154-a690cae0ed7e" "answer-b56f736f-e3af-523b-9386-b38726eccfb7" "answer-4c86e4c7-bde7-518f-883c-d6733741d589" "answer-33773b31-42d5-5b22-b69d-d51cba57075a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90c0ac77-fbcb-52ce-b243-0e75ec356a91" :answer/id #uuid "90c0ac77-fbcb-52ce-b243-0e75ec356a91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38d68fa8-7de7-5c1e-a154-a690cae0ed7e" :answer/id #uuid "38d68fa8-7de7-5c1e-a154-a690cae0ed7e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b56f736f-e3af-523b-9386-b38726eccfb7" :answer/id #uuid "b56f736f-e3af-523b-9386-b38726eccfb7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=5,$ and $c=2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c86e4c7-bde7-518f-883c-d6733741d589" :answer/id #uuid "4c86e4c7-bde7-518f-883c-d6733741d589" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=7,$ $b=2,$ and $c=3.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33773b31-42d5-5b22-b69d-d51cba57075a" :answer/id #uuid "33773b31-42d5-5b22-b69d-d51cba57075a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=7,$ $b=3,$ and $c=2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-abe1f7cc-b85d-54fe-b1a8-40e514a9b913"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-abe1f7cc-b85d-54fe-b1a8-40e514a9b913" :question/id #uuid "abe1f7cc-b85d-54fe-b1a8-40e514a9b913" :question/math-academy-id "q-81906" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}$ and $C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7ce86fa9-4f61-508b-a2b4-29ba44037897"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ce86fa9-4f61-508b-a2b4-29ba44037897" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce86fa9-4f61-508b-a2b4-29ba44037897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cd2f609f-5e0d-57ff-8535-d184b581b518" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4ec21016-7e3e-5b63-aa64-0f06d50c5c24" "answer-d0371c07-7afd-5a4b-8c13-05d0d0aba1ff" "answer-58f4f17b-6aad-55e7-b1d1-014e99ce6351" "answer-13aa3648-2a29-5251-b63d-d4b7f8b86f0c" "answer-cd2f609f-5e0d-57ff-8535-d184b581b518"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ec21016-7e3e-5b63-aa64-0f06d50c5c24" :answer/id #uuid "4ec21016-7e3e-5b63-aa64-0f06d50c5c24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0371c07-7afd-5a4b-8c13-05d0d0aba1ff" :answer/id #uuid "d0371c07-7afd-5a4b-8c13-05d0d0aba1ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58f4f17b-6aad-55e7-b1d1-014e99ce6351" :answer/id #uuid "58f4f17b-6aad-55e7-b1d1-014e99ce6351" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13aa3648-2a29-5251-b63d-d4b7f8b86f0c" :answer/id #uuid "13aa3648-2a29-5251-b63d-d4b7f8b86f0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd2f609f-5e0d-57ff-8535-d184b581b518" :answer/id #uuid "cd2f609f-5e0d-57ff-8535-d184b581b518" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186247362 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{e}_{1},{e}_{2}\\}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{17}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{17} & -\\frac{4}{17} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=17{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{4}{17}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 17\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-17862355-72a6-5fb8-967b-537e8771192b" :activity/id #uuid "17862355-72a6-5fb8-967b-537e8771192b" :activity/title "The Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050008 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-14b98c73-3778-5d7d-bd8f-d1f3c481dbdf" "step-047626a4-44a1-518d-a9b8-705468162bf9" "step-2efe5e7e-1009-5580-b1b3-51c076c0ff38" "step-c97d309f-4d0e-5752-9011-015efc23599f" "step-43b3afd1-0b21-5d59-9760-22f54f558dd0"] :activity/first-step "step-14b98c73-3778-5d7d-bd8f-d1f3c481dbdf" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-14b98c73-3778-5d7d-bd8f-d1f3c481dbdf" :step/id #uuid "14b98c73-3778-5d7d-bd8f-d1f3c481dbdf" :step/conte